EXT. WINDMILL RANCH - WATER TROUGH - DUSK

A tall iron windmill creaks in the failing light. A trough of dark water, a hitching post, a scatter of sleeping cattle on the hill.

SHERIFF BARNABAS TUCK, 60s, stands with his hat in his hand, watering his horse. His badge is dull, his boots caked.

He looks across the pasture. Far down the road, a lantern bobs.

TUCK
(to the horse)
Visitors.

The horse nickers. Tuck settles his hat and draws the rifle from its scabbard. The lantern grows. A buckboard emerges from the dusk, driven by OLIVE STARKEY, 40s, in a man's duster and a flat brim, a shotgun across her lap.

She reins up twenty feet away.

OLIVE
Evening, Sheriff.

TUCK
Olive.

OLIVE
You're off your range.

TUCK
So are you.

OLIVE
Not by my reckoning. This is my husband's ground.

TUCK
Your husband's dead.

OLIVE
Then it's mine.

Wind in the iron blades. The cattle shift. Tuck lowers the rifle a few inches.

TUCK
(to the hills, tired)
I'm too old for this.

OLIVE
You were too old ten years ago.

TUCK
Is that why you came? To tell me so?

OLIVE
I came to ask you to leave.

TUCK
I'll leave when I'm done.

OLIVE
Done with what?

TUCK
Looking at the water.

She studies him a long moment. Then, slowly, she lays the shotgun in the bed of the buckboard and climbs down. She walks to the trough, kneels, and drinks from her cupped hands.

OLIVE
It's good water.

TUCK
It's the best in the county.

OLIVE
He dug that well himself.

TUCK
I know.

She stands, wiping her palms on her duster. Tuck holsters the rifle.

OLIVE
Stay for supper.

TUCK
Is that an order?

OLIVE
It's an apology.

He smiles for the first time. The windmill turns, steady and patient, in the gathering dark.
